    chore(toolchain): raise Go floor to 1.26.11 (GO-2026-6809, GO-2026-6859)
    
    The row was written on 2026-11-04, when 1.26.10 was sufficient; it no longer
    is. go.mod declared `go 1.26.9`, and that stdlib carries two known
    vulnerabilities: GO-2026-6809 (crypto/x509 compromised intermediate CA, fixed
    in 1.26.10) and GO-2026-6859 (net/http, fixed in 1.26.11). "govulncheck
    reports 0 vulnerabilities" is only reachable at >= 1.26.11, so the floor is
    1.26.11, not the row's original 1.26.10 target.
    
    Evidence, measured in this worktree with a plain environment (no GOTOOLCHAIN
    override, so GOTOOLCHAIN=auto resolves the floor toolchain):
    
      - at 1.26.9:  govulncheck ./... reported exactly those 2 stdlib findings
                    (nothing in imported packages or modules)
      - at 1.26.11: go env GOVERSION = go1.26.11
                    make build => rc 0
                    go version -m ./off-by-one | head -1 => "./off-by-one: go1.26.11"
                    ~/go/bin/govulncheck ./... => "No vulnerabilities found." (rc 0)
                    make test => all 15 packages ok, zero failures
                    go vet ./... => clean
    
    No source edit, dependency bump or go.sum change is involved: the go.mod floor
    alone drives GOTOOLCHAIN=auto to the already-cached
    golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.26.11. No `toolchain` directive was added on
    purpose — the go.mod floor must stay the single source of truth that local
    builds and CI share.
    
    CI now pins exact patch versions everywhere (the matrix plus the deploy-gate,
    binary-seed-probe and golangci-lint jobs): a floating '1.26' resolves to
    whatever patch ships that week and can silently drift back below a security
    floor. README prerequisites and the bootstrap tarball move to 1.26.11, and the
    release procedure documents the reproducible prefix
    `GOTOOLCHAIN=go1.26.11 make release TAG=vX.Y.Z`, verified with
    `go version -m ./off-by-one | head -1`.
    
    Addresses RELEASE-OB-019.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
